# Worktree procedure — <project>

## One path, no tiers

<!-- There is no per-task tier decision. A worktree is born with its full
     isolated stack — its own database on its own port, its own services, its
     own network and volume, and its own runnable E2E — always. `--no-infra` is
     the explicit opt-out for a code-only checkout.

     CONTRACT: isolated E2E capability is included by default, not decided per
     task. `worktree_up` must leave a runnable per-worktree test-e2e.sh (template
     DB clone + namespaced E2E ports) with no additional ask — do not ask whether
     to include it.

     State the project-specific version of that sentence here: which services,
     which port band, what "full" means in this repo. -->

## Topology and configuration

<!-- The raw facts from scripts/detect-topology.sh, plus the human summary
     (single repo / split-git services + unversioned wrapper / classic
     multi-repo / …). Do not assume this generalizes to other projects —
     topology is detected per-project, never inherited as a default.

     Then the project's one input file, .context/worktrees/config.env: the dev
     ports, the worktree port band and stride, WT_LINKS / WT_COPIES, and any
     value that breaks a family convention (a database user that is not
     <project>_user, a port band chosen to avoid a neighbour). Record WHY, not
     just the value — the value is already in config.env. -->

## Participants & scope

<!-- Which directories/repos participate in worktree work at all (some may never
     need one — e.g. a docs-only sibling repo). The branch-naming convention used
     when work spans more than one participant, so they stay coordinated. Only
     touched participants get worktreed — never "all or nothing."

     Name anything deliberately OUT of WT_PARTICIPANTS and why: a component whose
     port the slot allocator cannot offset belongs here, and it is the reason for
     a row in ## Never run here below. -->

## Lifecycle & cleanup

<!-- Ephemeral (spin up -> run -> auto-teardown) or persistent (iterate for
     hours/days -> explicit teardown on exit).

     What exactly `worktree.sh down` must leave behind and what it must not:
     isolated DB dropped, isolated compose project/containers/network stopped,
     images built for the worktree removed (--rmi local), anonymous volumes
     reclaimed (NEVER shared named volumes), allocated slot freed, worktree
     directory kept or removed.

     "Dangling is not disposable" — a dev DB volume whose containers were removed
     by `compose down` still holds real data; never `docker volume prune -a`.
     See aidex-conventions/references/worktree-conventions.md for the full safety
     doctrine.

     How many worktrees can run at once (WT_MAX_SLOTS) and how collisions between
     concurrent sessions are avoided. -->

## Procedure

<!-- The EXECUTABLE side of the decisions above: exact commands, not prose. This is
     what makes later runs progressively simpler — an executor (or a future session)
     follows these steps verbatim instead of re-deriving them.

     Read state before acting:

       WT=~/.claude/skills/aidex-worktree/scripts/worktree.sh
       bash $WT list --porcelain

       bash $WT new  <slug> --branch <branch>   # checkouts + stack + migrate + E2E
       bash $WT up   <slug>                     # bring an existing stack back
       bash $WT down <slug> [--keep-dir]        # full teardown

     `new` is transactional — a failed step rolls the whole thing back — and it
     allocates the first free slot itself. `--force` discards uncommitted work and
     is NEVER the agent's decision.

     Add only what those commands do not cover for THIS project: a base-branch
     caveat, a `pnpm install` a fresh checkout needs, the gate commands run from
     the worktree root against its own stack. Refine after each real use (see the
     Usage log below). -->

## Running this worktree

<!-- Two questions a person actually has, answered as commands. Generate this from
     the derivation, not from memory: the start-at-create list is WT_SERVICES from
     config.env, and the on-demand services are the profile-gated ones that are in
     neither WT_SERVICES nor WT_SERVICES_BY_HOOK.

     Ports are read from the slot, never written as literals — the whole point is
     that these commands work unchanged on any slot. -->

| I want to… | Command (from the worktree root) |
|---|---|
| bring the stack up | `bash $WT up <slug>` — starts: `<WT_SERVICES>` |
| run the frontend | `<the resolved command; it must read the slot's port>` |
| run the E2E suite | `./test-e2e.sh` |
| start an on-demand service | `docker compose --profile <p> up -d <svc>` |

where `WT=~/.claude/skills/aidex-worktree/scripts/worktree.sh`, as in ## Procedure above.

Ports for this slot are in `<worktree>/.env`. Every command above reads them.

